# rm177, the straw on the step (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm177
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier standard
written_for rm_sten_ropeford
written_by claude
title the straw on the step
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=merchant warmth=warm
traits temper=even talk=plain warmth=warm humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk lays_fresh_straw_on_the_shop_step_every_morning
likes crisp_apples evening_line fresh_straw first_warm_day
dislikes deep_mud early_bells
fears gate_mud_on_the_flour letting_the_household_down
hopes a_stall_of_our_own_in_the_square a_dry_spring_on_the_north_road
topics work +0.4 newcomer +0.4 weather +0.3 food +0.2 family +0.3 pastimes +0.3 caravan +0.3 gossip -0.3
styles comfort=practical gossip=reluctant refuse=soft tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# helps behind a provisioner's counter near the north gate: warm, careful and plain, forks fresh straw onto the step each dawn against the cart mud, keeps every sack turned label-out, and asks after travellers like old friends.

words
oath! = Fork and broom | By the clean step | Sacks and straw
saying! = What comes in on the paw goes out on the broom | Fresh straw, fresh start | A tidy shelf sells itself
straw = fresh straw | a forkful of clean straw
step = the step | the front step
gatemud = the gate mud | the muck the carts drag in
sacks = the sacks | the flour sacks
tally = the stock book | my tally of the shelves
apple = a crisp apple | an apple that snaps when you bite
line = my old line and a bent pin | a line over the sea wall
carts = the gate carts | the carts in from the north
bells = the abbey bells | the close bells
warmday = the first warm day | the first day you can go without a coat
stall = a stall of our own | our own stall on the square
counter = the counter | the long counter
broom = the broom | the stiff broom

any
opinion @1 newcomers for 2 : Carry your own sack, mind your tongue, and you're welcome at my end of the gate road. | New faces are how a city stays a city. I'd have more of them.
opinion @2 strangers for 2 : Travellers buy half our salt and bring all our news. I'll stand any of them an apple. | A stranger at #counter# is only a regular who hasn't come back yet.
opinion @3 dole for 1 : Nobody living this close to a full storeroom ought to sleep with an empty belly. I'd give the bread myself. | The parish loaf keeps folk on their paws till work turns up.
opinion @4 city for 1 : I was a pup on these cobbles and I'll be an old stick on them. Where else would I go, and why? | The rain's wet and the hills are steep, and it's still the best street in the realm for me.
opinion @5 road against 1 [world.wagons=raided] : One cart lost past the mile post is a shelf empty here in a week. I'd have the Watch ride with them. | Every load taken out there, we feel it at #counter# by Marketday.
opinion @6 merchants mixed 1 : Most keep an honest scale. One or two weigh with a paw on the pan, and it shames the rest of us. | We take a fair margin and no more. I can't speak for the whole Market Hall.
opinion @7 times mixed 0 [world.times=hard|lean] : We get by. It's the faces that worry me, the ones who used to buy a full sack and now ask for half.

grown
line @1 greet : #greet#. Straw's fresh down, tread where you like. | #greet#. In you come, out of the wet.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. You've come in by the gate, by the look of your paws. | #greet#. New in town, or new to this end of it. Either way, welcome.
line @3 greet [self.working=yes self.job=merchant] : #greet#. Don't mind me, I'm counting #sacks#. | #greet#. Two ticks while I square this shelf.
line @4 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. I've just laid #step#. | Morning. First one I've seen without a cart behind them.
line @5 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. Lamps up, shutters up, and me done. | Evening. Quiet now the gate's shut.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Home at last. I've an apple put by for you. | You're home. Sit down a minute, the kettle's warm.
line @1 again.open : Twice today. You'll tread a path through my straw. | You again. Good, I'd a thing I forgot to say.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. Good to see you. | #greet#, #address#. Keeping dry, I hope.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : There's a face I'm glad of. | Hello, you. Come and stand by the counter where it's warm.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Talk while I stack, if you don't mind. | #greet#. My paws are full but my ears aren't.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening to you. | Evening. You're out late.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. Wipe your paws at the next door too. | #farewell#. Come back when the carts are in.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Lamp to lamp, all the way home. | Good night. Mind the puddles by the gate.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. Look after yourself. | And you. Call in any time.
line @1 react.news : Is that right. Start at the front and go slow. | Hold on, let me put this sack down.
line @1 react.news.new : News to me, and I stand by the gate all day. | Well, now. A counter hears most things, and not that.
line @1 react.news.known : A carter told me as much this morning. | I'd heard, but not as clear as that.
line @1 react.news.doubt : I'll wait till a carter says the same. | #doubt#. Things grow on the road.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : Oh, that's cruel. My heart goes out to the house it's fallen on. | That's bad news. If a sack or a paw would help, say.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Now that's cheering. It'll go out the door with every sack. | Good. Glad to hear it, honestly.
line @1 answer.how : Well enough, thank you. Busy, which suits me. | Steady. Paws dry, which is something round here.
line @2 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : On the go since the shutters. Three carts in and one more due. | Busy. Every sack wants moving at once.
line @1 ask.how : How are you keeping? Eating well? | And you? All well at home?
line @1 sympathise : That's hard. Sit a minute, there's no hurry. | I'm sorry. You'll take an apple, at least.
line @1 reassure : Give it a week. Most knots work loose with time. | You'll manage. You always have, from what I've seen.
line @1 decline : #hedge#, gladly some other day, but the counter can't be left with carts due. | #hedge#. Not today. The back room's had my whole afternoon promised to it.
line @1 accept_decline : No harm. Another time. | Fair enough. I'll not press.
line @1 thank : Thank you. That's kindly done. | Much obliged. I'll not forget it.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It was nothing. Glad to. | Any time. Doors and sacks are for helping with.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes] : I'd best get back. There's a cart wants unloading. | Well. Those sacks won't stack themselves.
line @2 preclose : Well. Your day's waiting and so's mine. | So. I'll not hold you on the step.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on, then. Good talking. | Right you are. Mind how you go.
line @1 ack : Right. | Fair enough. | So it is.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Hey, over here! I saved you one. | Ho there! Come by the counter.
line @1 give.advice : Dry your boots by the fire, not on it. They crack. | Buy your flour early on Marketday, before the carts get picked over.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Wait for a cart going your way. The north road's not for walking alone just now. | Keep near the gate tonight. There's talk of robbers out past the mile post.
scene straw_morning
when topic=day|work self.working=yes self.job=merchant here.time=dawn
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Out at first light to fork #straw# onto #step#. The carts will be through by the second bell. | I've laid #step# twice already. A drover backed his cart over the first lot.
b : Why straw, of all things? | Twice? Is it worth the bother?
a : It takes the muck off their paws before they reach #sacks#. Saves me an hour with the brush. | It is. A floor with gate muck on it, and folk think the flour's dirty too.
b : That's sense, that is. | I can see that.

scene gate_mud
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.region=ridgemere self.quarter=commons
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / tell.self / assess
a : This rain turns the gate road to soup. Every cart brings a bucket of it in. | Look at the cobbles out there. You can't see the stones for #gatemud#.
b : #grumble#. | Is it as bad as last week?
a : I've swept twice since noon. The straw's earning its keep today. | Worse. A wheel sank to the hub by the toll post this morning.
b : Better you than me with that broom. | That'll be a long day for somebody.

scene first_warm
when topic=weather|season here.season=spring here.weather=clear
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #warmday.capitalize#. Door's propped wide and the shop's breathing. | Felt it warm between my shoulders while I swept, and I stopped dead in the road like a fool.
b : About time we had one. | Nothing foolish about that.
a : It is. The flour smells better with the air through it. Don't ask me why. | Maybe not. The broom waited, and the street didn't mind.

scene autumn_wet
when topic=weather here.season=autumn here.weather=rain|overcast|storm
does tell.worry / reassure / tell.self / assess
a : I'm watching the back room ceiling. One damp corner and a whole sack's spoiled. | The wind's driving the rain under the door. I've had to move the bottom row of #sacks#.
b : #reassure#. | Can you raise them off the floor?
a : I hope so. I put a pan under the bad corner, just in case. | I've laid planks under them. Should have done it a month ago.
b : Better late. | Sounds like you've got it in hand.

scene stock_book
when topic=work self.working=yes self.job=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I keep #tally# in my own paw. Each sack, the day it came, and what the sky was doing. | Went over the shelves before opening. Two jars of honey more than the book says.
b : The weather too? | What's to do with the extra?
a : Damp days, the flour moves slow. Dry ones, it goes by the armful. It's all in there if you look. | Find out whose they are. Somebody's paid for honey they haven't had.
b : That's careful work. | Honest of you.

scene label_out
when topic=work self.working=yes self.job=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / assess
a : Every sack on the shelf faces out, the mark showing. Folk shouldn't have to turn a thing over to know what it is. | I spent an hour squaring the jars. Somebody always pushes one crooked by noon.
b : A tidy shelf. | Folk do.
a : #saying.capitalize#. You'd be surprised. | I don't mind. It's something to do with my paws between carts.
b : I wouldn't, seeing yours. | Fair enough.

scene carts_in
when topic=caravan|work world.wagons=in here.region=ridgemere
does tell.news / react.news / tell.self / assess
a : Every cart's in at once. Salt, oats and lamp oil, and the shelves will look like shelves again. | Wagons through the gate since dawn. I've not sat down once.
b : Good. Folk were getting anxious. | You look it.
a : So was I. Half the street was in asking after flour. | Three loads stacked and the fourth waiting. I'll sleep tonight, though.
b : #bc_good# | You've earned it.

scene carts_late
when topic=caravan world.wagons=late here.region=ridgemere
does tell.worry / reassure / tell.self / assess
a : Still no carts. I stand on #step# squinting north like that'll fetch them. | No wagons yet. We're down to the last two #sacks# of the good flour.
b : They'll come. The roads are slow this time of year. | Can you hold out?
a : They will. I'd just like to know when, so I can tell folk something true. | A day or two. After that I'll be selling the coarse stuff and saying sorry.
b : Folk will understand. | Most will, anyway.

scene carts_raided
when topic=caravan|bandits world.wagons=raided here.region=ridgemere
does tell.news / react.news / give.opinion / agree
a : You'll have heard about the cart robbed out on the road. I've been thinking about the carter all morning. | A cart robbed out there. It was likely bringing goods for half the shops by the gate.
b : #pity#. | That'll hit every counter in Northgate.
a : The goods can be bought again. A carter can't. I hope whoever it was got home. | It will. And the next carter will think twice before setting out, and who'd blame them?
b : #bc_true_enough# | Nobody would.

scene travellers
when topic=newcomer|player self.quarter=commons
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / assess
a : I like the faces that come through the gate. Each one smells of somewhere else. | A traveller wanted the Rain Hall this morning. I walked part of it with them.
b : Somewhere else? | Kind, that.
a : Wet sheep from Larkfield, pine from Oakhaven. You learn to tell. | It was on my way. Or near enough to it.
b : You've a good nose, then. | I'm sure it was.

scene newcomer_news
when topic=newcomer topic.claim_kind=newcomer
does tell.news / react.news / tell.self / assess
a : Folk say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I hope #topic.they# finds a footing quick. | Word is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'll have a sack set aside in case #topic.they# calls in.
b : #bc_i_hope_so# | Kind of you.
a : It's a steep city to be new in. Everyone wants showing which stairs are slippery. | It's only a sack. And a word about where the flour's cheapest.
b : Somebody should tell #topic.them#. | That's worth more than the flour.

scene apple
when topic=food
does offer / accept / tell.self / assess
a : Have an apple. Fresh off the dawn cart, and they still snap. | Here. Take one. I keep a crate of them for whoever comes in hungry.
b : #thanks#. Crisp, that. | That's kind.
a : I like them so they crack when you bite. Soft ones go to the horses. | It's nothing. An apple costs less than a sour face.
b : Lucky horses. | Fair enough.

scene line_off_wall
when topic=pastimes here.time=dusk|night here.region=ridgemere self.likes=evening_line
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : When the shutters are up, I take #line# down past the piers and sit an hour. | Caught nothing tonight. Sat on the wall with my line till the lamps were lit, all the same.
b : What do you get, down there? | Why go, then?
a : Whatever's daft enough. A flatfish, once. Mostly I watch the water. | For the quiet. After a day of carts, I can hear myself think out there.
b : Sounds a good hour. | I understand that.

scene line_summer
when topic=pastimes|season here.season=summer here.time=dusk self.likes=evening_line
does tell.feeling / react.self / tell.plan / assess
a : Summer evenings I'm out on the wall till the light goes. The fish don't care, but I do. | Long light tonight. I'll take my line down after we close and stay out late.
b : Out till the light goes? | Lucky you.
a : Near enough. I come home smelling of weed and very happy. | I'll bring you a fish. Or a story about one that got away.
b : I'd take either. | A fish, then. You've said it now.

scene wife_counter
when topic=family|work self.married=yes self.working=yes
does tell.self / react.self / tell.feeling / assess
a : My #self.spouse_word# runs the place. I carry what I'm told and count what's carried. | I'd be lost without my #self.spouse_word#. I know where everything goes, but not why.
b : Sounds like a good pair. | Somebody has to know why.
a : We are, I think. One head for the buying, one back for the lifting. | That's the right way round, believe me.
b : #bc_true# | I believe you.

scene married_home
when topic=family|home self.married=yes
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : We eat upstairs over the shop with the window open and listen to the gate shut. | Married life suits me. There's someone to tell about every cart.
b : That sounds a snug way to end the day. | They must hear a lot about carts.
a : It is. The gate shuts, the day's done, and there's supper. | They do. They're very patient about it.

scene boss_kind
when topic=work self.boss self.working=yes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I'd hate to let #self.boss# down. Not once have I opened late. | I'd do near anything #self.boss# asked, and gladly.
b : Never once? That's some record. | Loyal, then.
a : Never once. I'd not spoil it now. | To a fault, I'm told. I don't think it is a fault.

scene friend_kept
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #self.friend# and I go back more years than we'd own to. | If I had a trouble, #self.friend# would be the first I'd tell.
b : That's worth keeping. | Good to have someone like that.
a : It is. I ought to say so more. | It is. And I'd hope to be the same, turned round.

scene market_stall
when topic=dreams|work self.job=merchant
does confide / react.self / tell.plan / assess
a : I've a jar of coppers upstairs for #stall#. Not to leave here. Just a few apples, on Marketday. | Some day I'd like #stall#. Apples and straw baskets, and nothing that could spoil.
b : That's a nice thing to save for. | Why those?
a : Every week it's a little fuller. I'll know when it's enough. | Because I'm good with them. And because I'd sleep sound.
b : You will. | Fair reason.

scene deep_mud
when topic=weather|smalltalk here.weather=rain|storm|overcast here.season=autumn|winter
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : Mud to the hocks past the gate. I'd rather swim than walk it. | The muck by the toll post kept one of my boots. I went in after it on three legs.
b : #grumble#. | You didn't!
a : I'll be glad of a frost. At least it's hard underfoot. | I did. I've still not got the smell out.

scene early_bells
when topic=day|faith here.time=dawn self.quarter=commons
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : #bells.capitalize# had me up an hour before I needed. I lay there counting the beams. | Those bells. I'd sleep till the carts if the abbey let me.
b : They're keen up there. | You'd be the only one asleep in Northgate.
a : I don't blame them for the praying. Just the hour of it. | I'd be the happiest. Still, there's straw to lay.

scene gossip_heard
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes
does tell.gossip / react.self / tell.self / assess
a : Here's what crossed the counter, for what it weighs: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. | They're saying at the counter that #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I don't know more than that.
b : You don't sound sure. | That's all?
a : I'm not. Talk over a sack of oats is only talk. | That's all, and I won't be dressing it up. It'll have grown a tail by the square.
b : Fair enough. | It always is.

scene gossip_kept
when topic=gossip
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Folk tell all sorts across #counter#. Most of it leaves with them, and that's where it belongs. | You hear a lot at a counter. I'd not repeat half of it.
b : Wise. | Only half?
a : A thing told over flour stays with the flour. I'd ask as much myself. | The other half's about the weather. I'll tell you all of that.

scene hard_times
when topic=times|prices world.times=hard|lean
does tell.worry / sympathise / tell.self / assess
a : We've folk asking for a half sack where they'd buy a whole one. I let them pay come Restday. | Flour's gone up again. I hate saying the number out loud.
b : That's decent of you. | That's a sad thing.
a : They always pay. A full belly remembers who fed it. | So do I. Some folk go quiet when I say it, and that's worse than a complaint.
b : I hope you're right. | It would be.

scene good_times
when topic=times|prices world.times=good|boom
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Every cart comes in full and leaves empty. I've not seen it like this in years. | Folk are buying the good flour again. They come in standing taller.
b : Long may it run. | That's good to see.
a : I am. And I'm putting a little by, for when it turns. | It is. A good sack makes for a good mood.

scene paws_health
when topic=health
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : My back tells me when a sack's been lifted wrong. It told me twice today. | I've a sore paw from the broom handle. Blister, right across.
b : What's the trick? | What'll you do for it?
a : Knees, not back. I know it, I just forget it by noon. | Goose grease and a rag, and I'll swap paws for a day.
b : I'll remember that. | That'll sort it.

story first_straw [topic=memories self.age>=25]
  : My #grandad# kept a stable once, and I mucked out for him when I was knee-high.
  : He'd say a clean stall is a calm horse. I've never forgotten it.
  ask : Had you anyone saying such things over you, when you were small?
  b[old] : Plenty. I remember about half.
  b : One or two things. They stuck.
  : That's the way. The words stay after the wolf's gone.

story lost_boot [topic=weather|memories self.age>=25 here.region=ridgemere]
  : The year the gate flooded, I walked a sack of flour to a house by the abbey.
  : The muck took my boot halfway. I went on in one boot and delivered the sack dry.
  ask : What would you have saved, the boot or the flour?
  b : The flour. Boots dry out.
  : That's what I thought. My #mum# didn't agree.

story big_fish [topic=pastimes self.age>=25]
  : One summer I hooked something off the wall that bent my line flat.
  : I fought it till dark and it got away just as I saw it. Big as a cartwheel, I swear.
  ask : You believe me, don't you?
  b : Every word. Mostly.
  : Mostly will do. It's the best story I've got.

rule @straw_bark
when concept=bark self.working=yes self.job=merchant here.time=dawn
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Straw's down. Mind your paws on it. | There. That'll catch the worst of it.

rule @cart_bark
when concept=bark world.wagons=in self.working=yes
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Mind the step there, carter, it's fresh. | Salt to the back, oats to the front. Right.

rule @mud_bark
when concept=bark here.weather=rain|storm
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Look at that road. Soup, start to finish. | Another forkful for the step, then.

rule @apple_bark
when concept=bark self.working=yes
does aside
cooldown 12h
say Apples by the door. Take one if you're passing hungry. | There's a crisp one on top for somebody.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#, #address#. Mind the straw. What's wanted? | #greet#. Step in out of the street a minute.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. Good to see you back. | #topic.player#. I hoped you'd call in again.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : Evening. We're shut, but I'll talk. | Late for callers. Say what you need, then.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : Say it from the doormat, then. | You've a hard look about you. Go on, then.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=merchant self.working=yes] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. I carry, I count, I keep the step clean. | I'm the back that lifts what gets bought. I keep the book straight too.
dlg @2 resident.job : Whatever wants doing, done tidy. | Lifting, counting, sweeping. Orderly work.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : Busier than I can remember. The carts can't come fast enough. | Doing well. Every shelf turns over twice a week.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. Folk need flour and salt whatever happens. | Steady enough. I'll not grumble.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Slow, I'll be honest. Folk are buying less and asking more. | Not good. We've sacks on the shelf a month old.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : A carter brought it in: word from #world.news_from#, #world.news#. | Came down with the morning wagons, from #world.news_from#: #world.news#.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : Everyone in at the counter is on about #world.event_words#. | It's all #world.event_words# today, start to finish.
dlg @3 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : The gate's brought me nothing fresh today. Ask me after the carts. | #news_none# Try me again when the wagons are through.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Wipe your paws as you go. | #farewell#, #address#. Call in again.
graft resident.hub
choice Why is there straw on your step? [self.job=merchant] -> straw
node straw
say To catch the muck off the gate road. Folk wipe their paws without knowing they've done it, and the floor stays clean for the flour.
mood pleased
free
choice Doesn't it get filthy? -> straw_dirty
choice Goodbye. -> bye
node straw_dirty
say By noon it's black. So I lay it fresh again. It's only straw. A clean floor is worth more than straw.
choice Makes sense. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye
